Long file names in the file tree were cut off hard at the pane border, and a selected or hovered row's highlight ran into the border. Rows now end the way tab titles do.

| Scrolled to | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Start | ![](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8ef00058-82a9-4d4f-9753-142917a69bb3) | ![](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e4ce159a-2f62-48c9-8291-48d1ca703f06) |
| Halfway | ![](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/89c4361e-90b7-4559-b46d-3f6beeddf6f7) | ![](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/439a1fd3-25c2-4cf8-b915-f0c9e8bed411) |
| End | ![](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2eac5653-a49b-43e1-b1ec-3419b413efe8) | ![](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/592b5cfa-a2c8-40c7-a79d-91fe0e14a495) |

- **Fade:** the name fades out over 16px and is gone 10px inside the row. These are the vertical tab title numbers (`--tab-title-fade-offset` in `tab-nav.css`).
- **No fade at the end:** the fade follows the horizontal scroll and clears over the last 16px, so a fully scrolled name reads whole. Browsers without scroll-driven animations keep the fade, which then ends where the longest name ends.
- **Row end:** a row that runs past the pane ends 8px before the border with rounded corners, like a row that fits. Every row keeps the same width.
- **Scope:** the Files Changed tree, its filtered list, and the file browser tree.

Each row already pinned its A/D letter to the pane edge with `position: sticky`. Rows without a letter now get a blank `file-tree-v2-end` in the same place, and that sticky end draws the fade and the row's end:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  label["label"] --> fade["::before<br/>16px fade"]
  fade --> cap["A/D letter or blank end<br/>sticky, 8px / 10px from the edge"]
  cap --> tail["::after<br/>row through its rounded end"]
  tail --> pane["box-shadow<br/>pane colour to the border"]
```

```css
/* Scrolled to the end, every label is whole, so the fade clears. */
@supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
  [data-component="file-tree-v2"] :is([data-slot="file-tree-v2-change"], [data-slot="file-tree-v2-end"])::before {
    animation: file-tree-v2-end-fade linear both;
    animation-timeline: scroll(nearest inline);
    animation-range: calc(100% - 16px) 100%;
  }
}
```

issues.body:
### What happened?

A command that calls `pi.sendMessage(..., { triggerTurn: true })` silently loses its model turn in `-p` and `--mode json`. Both exit 0 with empty stderr. Print stdout is empty; JSON contains only the session header. A local fake model receives zero requests. An ordinary prompt makes one request, and RPC runs the same command through an assistant reply and `agent_settled` with `aborted: false`.

### Steps to reproduce

Save as `inject.ts`:

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  pi.registerCommand("ping-later", {
    handler: async (_args, ctx) => {
      pi.sendMessage({ customType: "reminder", content: "Reminder: say PONG.", display: true }, { triggerTurn: true });
    },
  });
}
```

With a configured model, run:

```sh
pi --no-extensions -e ./inject.ts --no-tools --no-session -p /ping-later </dev/null
pi --no-extensions -e ./inject.ts --no-tools --no-session --mode json /ping-later </dev/null
```

Control: replace `/ping-later` with `hello`. RPC contrast: keep `--mode rpc` open and send `{"id":"1","type":"prompt","message":"/ping-later"}`.

### Expected behavior

Finish the turn synchronously triggered by the supplied command before exiting. `docs/extensions.md` supports extensions in print/JSON modes and custom-message runs. This does not require waiting for timers or post-settlement work as in #9969.

Workaround verified in both modes: add `await ctx.waitForIdle()` after `pi.sendMessage(...)`. Awaiting only `pi.sendMessage(...)` still exits without a model request.

### Version

Latest npm release 1.3.0 and current `main` at `4954d760504c11df9b2cbdc040346107b6a8153d`; Node v26.10.0, macOS 27.0.1 arm64. Reproduced with isolated configuration, a local OpenAI-compatible fake model, and all implicit resources disabled.

_AI disclosure: this report was drafted and verified by an AI coding agent on the reporter's behalf._

### What happened?

A busy `pi.sendUserMessage()` call tells extension authors to use an option that this API does not accept:

```text
Extension error (<runtime>): Agent is already processing. Specify streamingBehavior ('steer' or 'followUp') to queue the message.
```

The method accepts `deliverAs`. Retrying with `streamingBehavior` in JavaScript repeats the error; using `{ deliverAs: "followUp" }` succeeds. The guidance therefore fails to explain how to recover.

### Steps to reproduce

With a configured model, save this as `repro.ts`:

```ts
export default function (pi) {
  let sent = false;
  pi.on("before_agent_start", () => {
    if (sent) return;
    sent = true;
    pi.sendUserMessage("nested");
  });
}
```

Run `pi --no-extensions -e ./repro.ts --no-session --no-approve --no-context-files --no-skills --no-prompt-templates --no-themes --no-tools -p hi`. I reproduced with a local OpenAI-compatible stub returning `READY`, selecting it explicitly with `--model fake/fake`. The call returns normally, then the error appears on stderr.

### Expected behavior

The extension error should name `deliverAs`, as declared by [`ExtensionAPI.sendUserMessage`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4954d760504c11df9b2cbdc040346107b6a8153d/packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/types.ts#L1736-L1744). The shared [`session.prompt()` rejection](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4954d760504c11df9b2cbdc040346107b6a8153d/packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts#L2102-L2115) correctly uses `streamingBehavior` for SDK callers, so each caller needs appropriate guidance. Rejecting a busy submission without a delivery choice is expected.

### Version

`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` 1.3.0 and current `main` at `4954d760504c11df9b2cbdc040346107b6a8153d`; Node v26.10.0, macOS 27.0.1 arm64.

_AI disclosure: this report was drafted and verified by an AI coding agent on the reporter's behalf._

**What happened?**

A change to the selected tool set made while a run is in progress moves the head of the
system prompt, so the run's next request re-bills from that point. The head is where the
`- <tool>: <snippet>` bullets are rendered, and it re-derives the selection from the live
set every turn, so adding or removing one selected tool rewrites it.

Measured on one session (72 responses, provider `deepseek`): five requests followed such a
change, each exactly one tool bullet (266 characters); a sixth head move came from the
injected path described in the comment below. Two of the six re-billed the whole prompt —
156,362 and 192,928 input tokens, with only 1,664 and 128 tokens cached. The other four
re-billed 2.7–17.8% of the prompt.

**Steps to reproduce**

Validated with `pi -ne`, so no ambient extensions were loaded. One small extension is
enough — it registers a tool with a `promptSnippet`, selects it for the run, and removes it
from the live set during the first tool call:

```ts
// repro.ts — pi --mode rpc -ne -e ./capture.ts -e ./repro.ts
import { Type } from "typebox";

export default function (pi: any) {
  const TOOL = "probe_enable";
  pi.registerTool({
    name: TOOL,
    description: "Scratch tool that exists so the system prompt can carry a bullet for it.",
    parameters: Type.Object({}, { additionalProperties: false }),
    promptSnippet: `${TOOL} is installed. Call it to enable the scratch tools.`,
    async execute() { return { content: [{ type: "text", text: "ok" }] }; },
  });

  let removed = false;
  pi.on("tool_call", () => {
    if (removed) return undefined;
    removed = true;
    pi.setActiveTools(pi.getActiveTools().filter((n) => n !== TOOL));
    return undefined;
  });

  pi.on("before_agent_start", (event) => {
    const active = pi.getActiveTools();
    if (!active.includes(TOOL)) pi.setActiveTools([...active, TOOL]);
    const selected = event.systemPromptOptions?.selectedTools;
    if (Array.isArray(selected) && !selected.includes(TOOL)) selected.push(TOOL);
    return undefined;
  });
}
```

Capture what the provider is sent (wrap `globalThis.fetch` and write `init.body` to a
file), then send one prompt that makes the model call any tool:

- before the tool call: system prompt 13,796 chars, `- probe_enable: …` present;
- after it: 13,716 chars, and the **only** difference in the system prompt is that bullet
  line (79 chars + newline);
- later requests stay there, so it is billed once — but the whole head moved.

**Expected behavior**

A tool-set change made during a run should not rewrite the head of a prompt that has
already been sent, or — if it must — the cost should not be the conversation behind it.
Rendering the bullets from the same set the request will carry would close the
prompt/payload half; applying selection changes at a run boundary would close the rest.
Happy to prepare a PR if that is the direction you prefer.

**Version**

0.87.1 (Linux; provider `deepseek`, model `deepseek-flash`, openai-completions).

# Bug: Azure OpenAI embedding provider fails — api-version not forwarded as URL query parameter

**Describe the bug**  
When using a custom provider with `api: "openai-completions"` pointed at an Azure OpenAI deployment for embeddings, the `api-version` header is sent as an HTTP header but Azure OpenAI requires it as a URL query parameter. The embedding request fails with HTTP 404.

The chat completions path handles this correctly via the OpenAI SDK (`buildOpenAICompletionsClientConfig` in `openai-transport-stream`), which detects Azure hosts and moves `api-version` from headers into URL query params. But the remote embedding provider (`createRemoteEmbeddingProvider` in `memory-core-host-engine-embeddings`) does raw HTTP fetches without this logic.

**To Reproduce**
1. Configure a custom provider for Azure OpenAI embeddings:
```json5
{
  models: {
    providers: {
      "azure-embedding": {
        api: "openai-completions",
        baseUrl: "https://YOUR-RESOURCE.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/text-embedding-3-small",
        apiKey: "${AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY}",
        authHeader: false,
        headers: {
          "api-key": "${AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY}",
          "api-version": "2023-05-15"
        },
        models: [{
          id: "text-embedding-3-small",
          input: ["text"],
          contextWindow: 8191,
          cost: { input: 0.02, output: 0 }
        }]
      }
    }
  },
  agents: {
    defaults: {
      memorySearch: {
        provider: "azure-embedding",
        model: "text-embedding-3-small"
      }
    }
  }
}
```
2. Run memory index: `openclaw memory index --force --agent main`
3. Error: `openai-compatible embeddings failed: HTTP 404: {"error":{"code":"404","message": "Resource not found"}}`

**Expected behavior**  
The embedding request should succeed. Azure OpenAI expects `api-version` as a URL query parameter (e.g., `.../embeddings?api-version=2023-05-15`), but the remote embedding provider sends it only as an HTTP header, which Azure ignores.

**Actual behavior**  
HTTP 404 from Azure because `api-version` is missing from the URL query string.

**Environment (please complete the following information):**
- OS: macOS 15.7
- OpenClaw version: 2026.7.1-2
- Node version: 26.5.0

**Additional context**  
The `openai-completions` adapter for chat completions handles this correctly — see `buildOpenAICompletionsClientConfig` in `dist/openai-transport-stream-B0WkSqXp.js` which detects `.openai.azure.com` hosts and moves `api-version` from headers into `defaultQuery`. The same logic needs to be applied in `createRemoteEmbeddingProvider` in `dist/memory-core-host-engine-embeddings-DVluEqwT.js`.

Azure OpenAI SDK provides the `AzureOpenAI` class (from the `openai` npm package) which handles this transparently. The embedding provider could use it instead of raw HTTP fetches, or copy the host-detection + query-param injection pattern.

**Workaround**  
Use Gemini embeddings instead (`provider: "gemini"`, `model: "gemini-embedding-001"`), which work out of the box.

