### Impact and severity

- Affected: Gateways with exec SecretRefs backed by a slow or rate-limited provider. A 1Password service account allows 1000 requests per day per account, shared by every service account in that account.
- Severity: high. One startup used the whole daily quota. After that, agent `main` sessions and the Control UI stayed unavailable, and any restart would fail at startup with exit 78, until the quota reset (~19 h).
- Frequency: observed once with full logs (2026-10-15). On 2026-09-02 the same daily limit ran out with about 484 of 1000 requests unexplained; I have not confirmed that it had the same cause.
- Consequence: provider quota exhausted, Gateway unusable.

### Additional information

- I have not confirmed what advanced the credentials revision in the live case. During the same window, the log shows the following for two other configured agents:
  - about one OpenAI OAuth refresh failure per minute: `OAuth token refresh failed for openai: Failed to fence every historical OAuth refresh peer. | Agent admission requires its host owner.`
  - `running startup:handler-prewarm` about 230 times per minute
  - about 2000 `SQLite read-only snapshot` lines per minute for those agents' databases
- Possible fix: share one `SecretRefResolveCache` across the passes of a single refresh call, so a retry re-reads auth stores but not providers. Also log each retry with which revision moved, and bound or back off the retries.
- Related:
  - #56217: crash-loop exhausts 1Password limits; a different path, through process restarts.
  - #168658: cold owners are never retried, and the 1Password resolver discards `op` stderr.
  - #171054: bounds the outer publication retry to 4 attempts; it does not touch this inner loop.
  - #70952 and #72278: dedup and memoization of provider lookups; both closed as not planned.

### Summary / Problem Statement
In `agent-auth-credentials.ts`, token credentials (e.g., Bearer tokens) are validated by comparing their expiration time against the current time:
```typescript
  if (cred.expires !== undefined) {
    const expires = asDateTimestampMs(cred.expires);
    if (expires === undefined || Date.now() >= expires) {
      return null;
    }
  }
```
This comparison is done without a grace period. If a token is extremely close to expiration (e.g. 1 second remaining), it is accepted. However, as soon as the agent starts executing its run (which can take minutes), the token expires, resulting in transient downstream authorization failures.

### Detailed Technical Context
- **Validation Check:** In `src/agents/agent-auth-credentials.ts`:
  ```typescript
  if (cred.type === "token") {
    if (cred.expires !== undefined) {
      const expires = asDateTimestampMs(cred.expires);
      if (expires === undefined || Date.now() >= expires) {
        return null;
      }
    }
    ...
  }
  ```

### Impact Analysis
- Unstable execution runs that fail mid-way with authentication errors when using short-lived tokens.
- Poor error feedback where expired tokens are accepted at startup but fail immediately after.

### Proposed Solution / Implementation Plan
1. **Grace Period:** Implement a minimum remaining lifetime threshold (e.g., 1 minute) before accepting a token:
   ```typescript
   const expirationThresholdMs = 60_000;
   if (expires === undefined || Date.now() + expirationThresholdMs >= expires) {
     return null;
   }
   ```
2. **OAuth Correlation:** Align this validation check with OAuth token refresh thresholds.

### Affected File References
- [src/agents/agent-auth-credentials.ts](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/main/src/agents/agent-auth-credentials.ts#L56-L68)

